Lobium — native pointer/hover media features for mobile personas.

WHY THIS PATCH EXISTS

`(pointer: coarse)`, `(hover: none)`, `(any-pointer: coarse)` and `(any-hover: none)` are how a page
asks "is this a touch device?", and they are among the first things a mobile-detection script reads.
Today an Android persona answers them correctly — but only because the sidecar sends
`Emulation.setDeviceMetricsOverride {mobile: true}` over CDP after the browser is up
(packages/engine-runner/src/mobile-emulation.ts).

That contradicts the product's own stated principle: the fingerprint is supposed to be established by
the BINARY before the first script runs, never by a CDP overlay. The CDP route is observably weaker
in three specific ways:

  1. It is a RACE. The commands are applied per target after `waitForEndpointOrExit` returns, so a
     document that runs `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)')` in a synchronous inline script at the very
     top of the first navigation can be served the desktop answer.
  2. It is per-target, so any target that appears without the installer having attached to it (a
     popup opened faster than the Target.attachedToTarget round trip) is a desktop-shaped tab in a
     mobile-shaped browser — an internal contradiction, which is worse than either answer alone.
  3. It requires a live CDP socket for the browser's whole lifetime. `mobileEmulation.close()` on any
     error path silently reverts every tab to desktop pointer semantics mid-session.

`Settings::GetPrimaryPointerType()` is the browser-side value DevTools' device emulation overrides.
Reading the persona config directly in MediaValues puts the answer in the renderer, before script,
with no socket and no race — the same shape as the device-width hook already in this file.

WHAT IT KEYS OFF

A TOUCH-PRIMARY persona: `ua_mobile` OR `ua_form_factor == "Tablet"`.

Both halves are needed. `ua_mobile` alone covers phones but MISSES ANDROID TABLETS, which set
`uaMobile=false` on purpose — real tablet Chrome omits the "Mobile" UA token, and
packages/fingerprint/src/android.ts asserts exactly that (`nav.uaMobile === (formFactor === 'phone')`).
A tablet persona would then report (pointer: fine) and (hover: hover) from the renderer while the CDP
layer said the opposite, which is a contradiction inside one browser and worse than either answer
alone. Real Android tablets report coarse/none.

`max_touch_points` is deliberately NOT the trigger, and neither is "has touch": a Windows laptop with
a touchscreen has touch points AND a genuine fine pointer with hover. Its form factor is "Desktop",
so it is correctly excluded.

The available-* variants report the coarse/none bit ONLY, matching real Chrome on Android, which
reports `any-pointer: coarse` and no fine pointer at all. Reporting both bits would describe a device
with a mouse attached — a combination that exists (a tablet with a trackpad) but is not what these
personas claim to be.

NOTE ON THE CDP PATH: it stays. Once this ships, the CDP commands become redundant for pointer/hover
but are still what supplies touch-event emission and the visual viewport, which are not media
features. `mobile-emulation.ts` should keep sending them.

diff --git a/third_party/blink/renderer/core/css/media_values.cc b/third_party/blink/renderer/core/css/media_values.cc
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/core/css/media_values.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/core/css/media_values.cc
@@ -404,16 +404,44 @@
   return frame->GetPage()->GetSettings().GetAcceleratedCompositingEnabled();
 }
 
+namespace {
+
+// True for a TOUCH-PRIMARY persona: a phone, or a tablet.
+//
+// ua_mobile alone is not enough. A tablet reports ua_mobile=false, because real tablet Chrome omits
+// the "Mobile" UA token - see the comment on ua_form_factor in lobium_fp_config.h. Keying only on
+// ua_mobile would leave every Android TABLET persona reporting (pointer: fine) and (hover: hover)
+// from the renderer while the CDP layer said otherwise, which is a contradiction inside one browser.
+//
+// A touchscreen LAPTOP is deliberately excluded: its form factor is "Desktop", and it genuinely has
+// a fine pointer and hover alongside its touch points.
+bool LobiumIsTouchPrimaryPersona() {
+  const lobium::LobiumFpConfig* cfg = lobium::LobiumFpConfig::Current();
+  return cfg && (cfg->navigator.ua_mobile || cfg->navigator.ua_form_factor == "Tablet");
+}
+
+}  // namespace
+
 mojom::blink::PointerType MediaValues::CalculatePrimaryPointerType(
     LocalFrame* frame) {
   DCHECK(frame);
   DCHECK(frame->GetSettings());
+  // Lobium: decided in the renderer before any script runs, rather than by a post-launch CDP
+  // override that a synchronous inline script on the first navigation can outrun.
+  if (LobiumIsTouchPrimaryPersona()) {
+    return mojom::blink::PointerType::kPointerCoarseType;
+  }
   return frame->GetSettings()->GetPrimaryPointerType();
 }
 
 int MediaValues::CalculateAvailablePointerTypes(LocalFrame* frame) {
   DCHECK(frame);
   DCHECK(frame->GetSettings());
+  // Coarse ONLY. Real Chrome on Android reports no fine pointer at all; adding the fine bit too
+  // would describe a phone with a mouse attached.
+  if (LobiumIsTouchPrimaryPersona()) {
+    return static_cast<int>(mojom::blink::PointerType::kPointerCoarseType);
+  }
   return frame->GetSettings()->GetAvailablePointerTypes();
 }
 
@@ -421,6 +449,11 @@
     LocalFrame* frame) {
   DCHECK(frame);
   DCHECK(frame->GetSettings());
+  // Lobium: a touch-primary device cannot hover. '(hover: hover)' on a persona that reports
+  // '(pointer: coarse)' is a direct self-contradiction, and that is what a detector looks for.
+  if (LobiumIsTouchPrimaryPersona()) {
+    return mojom::blink::HoverType::kHoverNone;
+  }
   return frame->GetSettings()->GetPrimaryHoverType();
 }
 
@@ -434,6 +467,9 @@
 int MediaValues::CalculateAvailableHoverTypes(LocalFrame* frame) {
   DCHECK(frame);
   DCHECK(frame->GetSettings());
+  if (LobiumIsTouchPrimaryPersona()) {
+    return static_cast<int>(mojom::blink::HoverType::kHoverNone);
+  }
   return frame->GetSettings()->GetAvailableHoverTypes();
 }
 
